  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="4737-d1c9-730e-ece2" name="Early German (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #41 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). 150 BC to 300 AD The German tribes first came to the attention of the Romans when the latter expanded north of the Alps. They were fierce warriors with a different social system to that of the Gauls, and proved to be an insoluble problem for Rome. The most famous clash between them came in the disaster of the Teutoberger Wald in 9AD, when the auxilia commander Arminius treacherously led three legions into a trap from which few escaped. For the duration of the Empire, the Rhine would mark the edge of the “civilised” world. Allies: A German army may take allies from the auxiliaries section of the Early Imperial Rome list, representing the Batavian involvement in the revolt of 69 AD.

ARMY COMPOSITION:
[Early German]
  Cavalry: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: at least 50%
  Skirmishers: up to 20%
  Characters: up to 25% Infantry: At least 50% Cavalry: Up to 25% Skirmishers: Up to 20% Allies: Up to 20%</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
</catalogue>
